WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.551
Film Courage: How did you spend your first
few years as an actor and then how do you

00:00:03.551 --> 00:00:04.770
spend them now?

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.770
Choice Skinner, Actor, Director and Acting
Teacher: Good question.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.510
My first few years as an actor was hard work.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:17.730
It was some…you know I told my actors the
story about eating eggs, water and donuts

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.050
but making sure I had money for my acting
class.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.380
I didn’t even have a car, but I would take
the bus to get there.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.609
I didn’t know how I would get home but God
blessed me with someone who would give me

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:28.609
a ride.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:31.739
It was all about just being the best that
I could possibly be.

00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:38.129
I wanted to be in the ranks of the leading
men of my caliber: Denzel, Wesley Snipes,

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:39.410
Cuba Gooding.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.539
Those were the scenes I worked on and I knew
who I was as an actor and I worked towards

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:45.940
playing those authoritative roles.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.579
I didn’t have a problem playing thugs or
you know, gangbangers.

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:50.949
I loved those characters.

00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:53.699
Those are the guys I grew up with so I understand
it.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:58.859
But I’ve never been jaded in the sense of
like not having the opportunity to play certain

00:00:58.859 --> 00:00:59.929
roles.

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:06.180
Listen, in my beginning as being an actor
I worked on as many roles as I possibly could.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.600
If the roles didn’t exist, I wrote them
and I would bring them into class.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.720
And so for me it was just being the best that
I could possibly be to understand the process

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:24.400
of acting and to be great at it and to be
a joy to be around because I understood that’s

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.130
how people continue to work with you.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:32.220
Where I am at now (as an actor), I really
don’t care for the auditioning process.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.220
I don’t care for it.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.040
I do it.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.851
My manager is always on me about it, so I
do it.

00:01:36.851 --> 00:01:38.200
I go in.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:45.610
One of the things that made me not care for
it is knowing the politics behind it because

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:48.850
I’m a producer and a director now.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:54.090
I know what I didn’t know back then when
I was just acting and that is sometimes even

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.320
if you’re auditioning for a role, you’re
already not going to get it because they may

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.990
be on the phone talking to the person that
they just offered the role to.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.430
So that’s kind of tough.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.410
Where I’m at now, I create my own content.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:08.410
I direct.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:12.640
I write and I have people who call me up and
put me in projects because they remember me

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.340
as an actor and they remember my work.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.990
So it can be a little funny sometimes for
me because it’s like listen do I really

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:25.890
want to waste the time and audition for something
that I may not get or do I just continue to

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:30.690
do the process because that’s what that
is, auditioning is part of the acting process.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.570
Yeah, I don’t know?

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:39.840
Where I’m at now, I just love having something
that I write or someone write for me say “Hey

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.840
let’s come do this!”

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.840
It’s easier.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.350
There’s no pressures, no stress about it
and I enjoy that a little bit more than yeah…I’ve

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.540
never been a competitive person.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.680
Even in the marital arts, fighting in tournaments
wasn’t for me.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.600
So with acting I don’t look at it as competition,
I look at it like they say as love.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.560
There can’t be competition in love.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.510
If the role is right for me, give it to me.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.600
I’ll show up for you and I’ll do a great
job.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:12.250
Film Courage: Do you think that audition grind
is what sends a lot people home or somewhere

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.250
else?

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.250
Choice Skinner: Somewhat.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.860
I think what sends people home mostly is lack
of money.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.760
People get into the entertainment industry
thinking that they’re going to make a lot

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.400
of money and I always tell actors if you got
into the entertainment industry to make money,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.400
you can do anything else.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.120
I mean you can do other types of movies and
make a lot of money.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:38.099
Acting…it’s a love/hate relationship for
some people because they may have gotten out

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:43.310
of college or they left a corporate job or
they left pharmaceuticals…I knew an actor

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:48.380
who was a rocket scientist but he wasn’t
happy unless he was doing acting.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:53.459
And they get into the acting world and they
want to make a lot of money and two, three

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:56.820
years go by, four years, maybe five and they’re
not making any money and they’re working

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:00.560
a dead-end job and they start to battle with
that.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.010
And so typically I think typically that’s
usually what sends people home.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:10.069
The auditioning process has a different experience.

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:16.650
When you hear these stories of like Angelina
Jolie or Mark Ruffalo, they say he went out

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:23.110
fifty times until he got his first role I
believe that everybody’s resilience is a

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.470
different level.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.309
I didn’t have to go out fifty times.

00:04:26.309 --> 00:04:30.630
I think I went out three times and I booked
my first audition.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:38.930
I was that type of actor but I could see how
the rejection…because like I said everybody’s

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:43.849
looking for love and even if you were going
out for somebody that you like or you’re

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:47.430
attracted to to and you get that rejection,
there’s a thing you go through.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.300
There’s a catharsis, there’s a hurtful
period you go through.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.669
And I think if you’re going for auditions
and you may be right for them and you are

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:57.240
being told “No, no, no…”

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.210
You start to question yourself “Is this
right for me?

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.009
Is this really what I should be doing?”

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:05.039
Listen, if we’re all climbing up a mountain,
we don’t know what is at the top of the

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:07.090
mountain until we get there.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.770
The climb is just part of the journey.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.110
Auditions are just part of the journey.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:17.129
And I do believe that there’s a point where
actors need to know if they are auditioning

00:05:17.129 --> 00:05:18.210
actors or not.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:25.050
Which basically means this, if you go in and
you cannot defeat the nervousness, maybe auditioning

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.050
is not for you.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.360
Unless it helps you to book the audition.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.830
Maybe being nervous books that audition?

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:34.909
Maybe you go in, you feel like you did a horrible
and then you get the callback and you book

00:05:34.909 --> 00:05:35.909
it?

00:05:35.909 --> 00:05:36.909
Like “I was horrible?”

00:05:36.909 --> 00:05:37.909
That happens.

00:05:37.909 --> 00:05:41.629
But I do believe that there’s a sense that
actors need to know if they are auditioning

00:05:41.629 --> 00:05:42.860
actors.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:47.199
Is this auditioning process for you and if
it isn’t what adjustment are you willing

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:50.479
to make in order to be great at it?

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:53.939
So that way you are not missing out on opportunities
or you’re not wasting your time.

